  But then `thi_q = tofOf(mzOf(last) + 1e-5, b)` at line 4116: mzOf(last) for edge cal = 1.1e8, tofOf(1.1e8 + 1e-5): u = sqrt(1.1e8) ≈ 10571 > u_half? u_half ≈ 10571 — right at it. For mz slightly above u_half², mzToTof still returns a value on... mzToTof is closed-form: t = C0 + b·sqrt(m) + C2·m. It's monotonic up to the vertex m_v = (b/(2|C2|))² - C4 = u_v² - C4 = (2·u_half)² = 4·u_half². So mzToTof is monotonic up to 4×u_half², beyond the half-slope point. mzOf(1e8)+1e-5 lands just above u_half² — still below vertex, monotonic, round trip consistent-ish. So thi_q ≈ 1e8. OK. But this shows for near-edge calibrations, tofOf receives m/z ≈ 1.1e8 > 1e5 — beyond the stated m/z domain, though still on the rising branch (below the vertex). Non-monotonicity only beyond vertex m_v = 4×u_half² ≈ 4.5e8 for this case. So no wrong output, but the "domain" story is more nuanced. Fine on real data (PXD: mzOf(N-1) ≈ 1700).

  Hmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: for near-edge accepted calibrations, mzToTof is non-monotonic beyond the vertex. Callers relying on monotonicity: tofOf saturates at 4294967294u (line 649). If mz > m_vertex, mzToTof returns decreasing t — a *smaller* TOF for a *larger* m/z. In `best`, hi_t = tofOf(cmz + 3sd): if cmz+3sd > m_vertex, hi_t collapses to a small value, and the window search misses peaks — silently wrong trace detection. But cmz ≤ 42950 (store cap) and for accepted calibrations m_vertex ≥ 4 × u_half² ≥ 4×1e5 = 4e5 > 42950? Wait u_half² ≥ 1e5 (m/z domain condition) → m_vertex = 4·u_half² - C4 ≥ 4e5 - C4. With C4 negative (observed -0.09), fine; with large positive C4? C4 is unbounded in unsupportedReason! There's no C4 check at all. C4 = -1e6: then u = sqrt(m + C4) requires m > 1e6; m/z up to 1e5 → m = mz + C4 < 0 → mzToTof uses sqrt(0) → t = C0 + C2·m with m negative large... and tofToMz at that t: u = 2(t-C0)/(b+sqrt(disc))... this is getting exotic. A negative-C2 file with C4 = -1e6: domain check 1e5 + C4 = -9e5 ≤ u_half² trivially true → accepted. But real peaks at m/z 100-1700 have m = mz + C4 < 0 → tofToMz returns NaN (m ≤ 0). Every peak NaN. Not plausible-wrong, just all dropped. The vendor oracle wouldn't produce such a file. Theoretical. Hmm, but is a large positive C4 a problem? C4 = 1e6: m/z domain check: 1e5 + 1e6 = 1.1e6 ≤ u_half² — stricter, fine. Real m/z 100-1700: m = 1e6+, u = sqrt(m) — the vendor formula would give the same. Domain check accounts for C4 ✓.

  But hold on — the m/z domain condition uses `kDomainMaxMz + C4 <= u_half * u_half`. If C4 is large positive, this correctly tightens. If C4 makes u_half² - C4 (the m/z at half-slope) less than... the check is correct. But what if u_half² overflows? covered earlier (accepted, fine since vertex at inf).

