  Because the denominator \(2C_2\) is negative, the sign of each root depends on whether the numerator is positive or negative.  
  I found that the root with the plus sign is negative, while the root with the minus sign ends up positive.Checking root signs...It analyzes a quadratic with C₂ negative and (C₀‑t) negative, finds that both roots are positive, and notes that the discriminant b²‑4C₂(C₀‑t) decreases as t increases, vanishing at t = C₀ + b²/(4|C₂|).Analyzing the quadratic roots for a negative \(C_2\), determining which branch is rising versus falling, and computing \(u\) using a discriminant‑based expression.Deriving the rising‑branch root \(u_{+}=\frac{2(t-C_{0})}{\,b+\sqrt{\text{disc}}\,}\) and confirming that the result is continuous as \(C_{2}\to0\), while also handling the special case of \(-0.0\).I note that b² won’t overflow, but t may become ∞ if the timebase is huge, and the code checks for NaN but not infiniteness, which could influence tofToMz.I note that setting t to ∞ makes the discriminant NaN, but the negative‑C2 branch rejects the case, and infinite digitizer delays are also flagged as invalid.Analyzes negative‑C₂ overflow: cf_hi→∞ makes b=0 so roots collapse, but cf_lo→–∞ triggers earlier rejection, confirming fail‑closed logic.I noted that lo may be a tiny positive value, b could underflow to zero if C1·cf_hi overflows, and that u_half² can overflow to infinity when C2 is an extremely small negative number—yet the domain checks still allow this case.I check that a very small negative C2 behaves like zero in floating‑point arithmetic, so u simplifies to (t–C0)/b. Then I work out the decision corner from the inequality disc ≥ b²⁄4, which gives t ≤ C0 + 0.75 b²/k, showing t_half depends on b², and note that the worst t comes from the maximum timebase plus delay, while the worst b comes from the smallest b².They verify that the temperature‑based scaling factor \(cf_{\text{hi}}\) is bounded by \(1+|dC1|\cdot1000/10^6\) and use \(|\cdot|\) for both signs. Then they confirm that enforcing \(k_{\text{DomainMaxMz}}+C4\le u_{\text{half}}^2\) guarantees \(\sqrt{mz+C4}\le u_{\text{half}}\) for every \(mz\le10^5\), the constraint being easier when \(C4<0\) andI look at the edge case where mz + C4 ≤ 0: sqrt returns zero, so t = C0 + C2·m, and if both C2 and m are negative t is greater than C0, making tofToMz use the falling branch. Inputs with mz ≤ 0 are basically garbage-non­physical. I verify that even for mz up to 1e5 the condition kDomainMaxMz + C4 ≤ u_half² still holds.I noted that u ≤ u_half ensures the slope constraint, with u_half = b/(4|C₂|) and the vertex at 2·u_half. The derivative at u_half equals b/2 and the discriminant there is b²/4. I began expanding t_half = C₀ + b·u_half + C₂·u_half² in terms of k = 4|C₂|, but the derivation stops midway.I checked the formulas and confirmed that \(t_{\text{half}}\) reduces to \(C_0+\tfrac34\,b^2/k\) and \(t_{\text{zero}}\) to \(C_0+b^2/k\), matching the earlier derivation. However, there’s a subtle issue: the acceptance condition \(t_{\max}\le t_{\text{half}}\land k_{\text{DomainMaxMz}}+C_4\le u_{\text{half}}^2\) is evaluated at the smallest \(b\), while \(t_{\max}\le t_{\text{half}}\) ought to hold for the entire \(b\)-range.t_half increases with b², so the tightest constraint comes from the smallest b, while u_half² also grows with b². The temperature check shows the smallest b occurs at the maximum C1 factor cf_hi, and we must ensure cf_lo remains positive over the full temperature range; a gap remains regarding the negative C2 case when the digitizer…Exploring the effect of a denormal timebase versus a huge negative delay on t_max vs t_half, noting that a massively negative delay forces t below C₀ and yields NaN results from tofToMz.The highest positive value produces conversions of NaN, while an extreme negative C0 causes the model to reject the input; the failure message may show “inf” or very large numbers.Calculated a t_zero of about 650 µs, found t_max > t_zero, so the model refuses the pair; the computed TOF index for when the root disappears turns out negative, producing a low‑severity “stops existing at TOF index –127328” message.I’m checking edge cases: ensure cf_hi never overflows by keeping cf_lo positive (|dC1|<1000), verify a denormal timebase still gives a reasonable t_max (~25,800 µs) but leads to infinite TOF in error messages, and ask whether any valid data row can have TOF ≤ 1e8 or m/z below a given limit.I plan to verify that if the maximum time t_max stays below the half‑time computed with the smallest b, then for any larger b the discriminant remains non‑negative, ensuring valid solutions. I note that this relies on t_half increasing with b², but I need to confirm the inequality holds across the entire range.I conclude that keeping the inequality \(b(T_1) \ge b_{\min}\) true for every temperature in the span makes the discriminant check sound. However, because the simulated \(b(T_1)\) is computed with floating‑point rounding, it could end up infinitesimally smaller than the worst‑case estimate, leaving a negligible risk that \(\mathrm{disc}\ge b^2/4\) fails.The gap between the check threshold of 0.25 and typical values near 0.98 is wide, so a one‑ulp change in b makes little difference. The logic that requires fabs(t1 − T1_ref) ≤ 1000.0 matches the domain span, and a frame exactly 1000 units above the reference still falls inside the allowed cf limits. Edge cases such as t1 = 1e308 or –∞ produce huge fabs values and are rejected, while a NaN t1 yields a NaN in the comparison.I verified that NaN comparisons are false, so a NaN T1 causes rejection, that extremely large differences overflow to infinity and are likewise rejected, and noted that it’s unclear whether a frame can reach a frameFactor when T1 is read unchecked because the loader accesses Frames.T1 directly.Reviewing section F (lines 153‑173) finds no frameReason call, so a negative C₂ file with a corrupt T1 (1100 °C) would still load via the OpenMS loader. The user wonders whether loadTdfCalibration is invoked consistently, noting that loadTofAxis runs only when band_edges_acq && integer_req; otherwise the axis data comes from elsewhere. The spectra are read through a patched BrukerTimsFile that converts m…Loader converts frames with negative C2 and corrupt T1 without validation, treating them as if they had positive C2; peaks beyond the parabola then become NaN and are dropped as bad mz, as noted in the header comment. The question is whether this behavior violates invariant 1 (fail‑closed semantics).The review notes that larger b values increase the discriminant, making conversions safer, but falling below T1_ref - 1000 K shrinks b and can lead to disc < b²/4, causing NaNs and dropped peaks outside the ±1000 K test window.The model may output wrong or NaN m/z for corrupt frames when T1 is outside the permitted range and C₂ is negative, but it isn’t fail‑closed; it simply degrades to NaN/drop and the header notes this. This scenario has medium theoretical severity, requiring a corrupt T1, negative C₂, and a loader‑only path. Even when the discriminant stays non‑negative, the conversion can seem plausible but is only an artifact of the model’s guarantees, not a failure in the physics.The risk from an unchecked or corrupted T1 is negligible unless dC1 is very large, because even extreme T1 values produce a plausible b and keep the discriminant near b². Negative‑C₂ calibrations are rejected when cf_lo ≤ 0, so the loader path prevents significant errors.With dC1 = 999 and T1 = T1_ref – 1 million, the shrinkage of b still leaves the discriminant comfortably above b²/4 for typical C2 values, so the root stays valid; only at the extreme edge would the discriminant approach the boundary.The Q4 answer is that the OpenMS loader can indeed process frames with unchecked T1 values; it falls back to a NaN‑drop behaviour that’s documented and bounded. The TofAxis::factor() fallback uses T1_ref within the allowed span, and the setTofAxis logic only defaults to the reference factor if the computed value is non‑positive.SetTofAxis uses t1 from loadTdfCalibration, so all frames stay in range for negative C2; for C2 ≥ 0 a NaN frameFactor makes the filter fail. mzPeak exact() also pulls t1_by_frame from the loader. Q3 points out that the OpenMS loader patch reads raw uint32 TOF indices up to ~4.3 × 10⁹, far above the 1 × 10⁸ limit, which may affect the DigitizerNumSamples bound.The loader doesn’t enforce the 1 × 10⁸ TOF limit, so a corrupt frame with a 6 × 10⁹ index on a negative‑C₂ file would yield a NaN m/z, and I’m checking whether the tool still drops that NaN peak and counts it as bad_mz, even though the loader only writes NaN into the spectrum.I see that in a real negative‑C₂ dataset (PXD029836) the TOF indices stay far below 1 × 10⁸, but even the maximum 32‑bit value yields a discriminant ratio of 0.4367, comfortably above the 0.25 threshold, so the half‑slope safety margin is satisfied even for extreme TOFs. However, the formal domain limit still caps TOF at 1 × 10⁸, which for a calibration with |C₂| = 0.0595 would give t ≈ 8.6 × 10⁸ ns at TOF = 4.29 × 10⁹.Computed c2_edge so that t_half equals t_max at a TOF of 1 × 10⁸; for a TOF of 4.29 × 10⁹ the discriminant is negative, yielding NaN that the loader still propagates. This only happens on corrupt data, and the tool actually rejects such a file only when loadTdfAxisBounds is invoked during the dnoise setup, which needs to be verified.The loader reads GlobalMetadata, but recoverPoint throws a refusal when a TOF bin exceeds 2^32. With dnoise enabled, a NaN m/z from the loader triggers this refusal path and causes the tool to throw an error. Without dnoise, the NaN m/z may slip into the spectra and rely on OpenMS to decide whether to drop it.I plan to grep diaspextract.cpp for a bad_mz counter and review TofAxis::tofOf callers to confirm that m/z limits stay within the expected bounds.I’m estimating the m/z value for a TOF of 1 × 10⁸ in an edge calibration. The calculation shows u_half squared could reach about 1.1 × 10⁸ m/z, far exceeding the 1 × 10⁵ limit, suggesting that such a calibration would produce an unrealistically high m/z at that TOF.I'm checking that the m/z condition holds even though mzOf(1e8) produces a very high m/z, and then inspecting the line 4116 expression for thi_q.I computed that at the edge calibration the monotonicity of mzToTof holds up to 4×u_half², so a round‑trip near 1×10⁸ remains consistent, giving thi_q ≈ 1×10⁸.For calibrations near the maximum allowed domain, the calculated m/z can reach roughly 1.1 × 10⁸—above the 1 × 10⁵ limit—yet the point still lies on the rising side of the parabola, so no immediate error occurs. The function becomes non‑monotonic only after the vertex (≈ 4.5 × 10⁸ in this example), so real‑world data remain safe, but callers that assume monotonicity may see the `tofOf` routine saturate at its 32‑bit maximum value.When m/z exceeds the parabola’s vertex, mzToTof returns a smaller time, collapsing the search window and causing peaks to be missed; however, accepted calibrations keep the vertex above the 42,950 m/z cap because u_half² ≥ 1e5, so this issue only arises if C4 is negative or otherwise unbounded.No C4 validation lets a highly negative C4 collapse any peak into NaNs, which seems unrealistic for real data.I’m checking whether a large positive C4 tightens the m/z domain check correctly, and noting that while it should make the inequality stricter, the calculation of u_half² could overflow.It notes that the mzPeak `exact()` method converts m/z to TOF via the two‑point inverse, which can produce very large TOF values that, if outside the allowed domain, cause a NaN and a fail‑closed throw. The frame temperature T1 is obtained from `loadTdfCalibration`. Additionally, `loadTdfAxisBounds` is called in the `MzPeakExactMz` constructor.Failure just marks the bounds_why flag instead of throwing an error, and the n_bins value shows up when constructing the axis. In dnoise’s recoverPoint, mzToTof is applied to every m/z from the loader—real peaks up to ~1700 or possibly corrupted values (up to the loader’s 4 × 10⁴ cap). If a value exceeds the vertex mass, mzToTof returns a very small time, which could round into an acceptable bin within the tolerance, causing recoverPoint to mis‑identify the point’s original TOF bin.A non‑monotonic inverse can return the wrong TOF for m/z beyond the vertex, leading to silent misassignment of m/z when the denoiser later re‑converts that bin.I’m checking if points beyond the parabola‑vertex would produce a time that, when rounded, meets the tolerance test, noting that the forward and inverse conversions give different m/z branches.Considering that tofToMz produces only rising‑branch m/z, mzToTof can safely invert those values with the rising‑branch formula and ignore the falling branch, which never occurs.The rising‑branch reaches a vertex TOF far beyond 2^32, so every realistic bin lies before the vertex; for a near‑edge calibration the vertex is ≈ 1.33×10⁸, and any higher corrupt bin (e.g. 4.3×10⁹) yields NaNs that recoverPoint refuses, which is already fail‑closed.  
