• Let me check line 730 context (the m/z validity gate that drops NaN peaks — confirms the "drops and counts (bad_mz)" claim applies to loader-converted peaks), and line 1370-1418 (the dnoise count). Also check CMake C++ standard and whether the tool's integer detector path drops NaN. Also check where loadTofAxis is called beyond the preflight (dnoise setup) and whether exportMz_ is the only place NaN m/z from mzOf is handled.

  Also check `diaspextract.cpp:730`: `mz * MZ_Q > 4.2e9` — the store cap. MZ_Q = 1e5? So cap = 4.2e9/1e5 = 42000 m/z ✓ matches "caps at 42,000".

  Let me view around lines 686-735 and check where in the pipeline this happens (which detector). And CMakeLists CXX_STANDARD.I will examine lines 730 and 1370-1418 to confirm how NaN m/z values are handled, check the C++ standard used by CMake and whether the integer detector path drops NaN, and verify where loadTofAxis and exportMz_ are called. I’ll also inspect the store capacity check at line 730 (`mz * MZ_Q > 4.2e9`, with `MZ_Q = 1e5`).I confirmed that the maximum m/z capacity is 42 000, matching the documented cap. Next I’ll inspect the source around lines 686–735 to see where in the pipeline this cutoff is enforced and check the C++ standard specified in the CMakeLists file.  constexpr double IM_Q = 65535.0 / (IM_HI - IM_LO);

  /// Peaks that cannot be represented exactly are DROPPED and COUNTED: clamping 1/K0 would stack
  /// outliers on two artificial mobilities, and NaN into llround is UB.
  struct CompactStats
  {
    size_t no_im_array = 0, size_mismatch = 0, bad_mz = 0, bad_im = 0, kept = 0;
    size_t unmapped_frame = 0;   ///< nativeID carried no parseable "frame=": cannot be calibrated
    /// merge per-frame stats after a parallel batch [perf-load]
    CompactStats& operator+=(const CompactStats& o)
    {
      no_im_array += o.no_im_array; size_mismatch += o.size_mismatch;
      bad_mz += o.bad_mz; bad_im += o.bad_im; kept += o.kept;
      unmapped_frame += o.unmapped_frame;
      return *this;
    }
  };

  /// The unsigned integer after `key` in a native ID; 0 when absent.
  inline uint32_t nidField(const String& nid, const char* key)
  {
    const size_t i = nid.find(key);
    if (i == std::string::npos) return 0;
    uint32_t v = 0; size_t k = i + strlen(key);
    for (; k < nid.size() && isdigit((unsigned char)nid[k]); ++k) v = v * 10 + (uint32_t)(nid[k] - '0');
    return v;
  }
  /// The vendor Frames.Id ("frame=", written by the .d loader and the mzPeak reader; "mzpeak=" is an archive index). 0 = unknown.
  inline uint32_t frameIdOf(const String& nid) { return nidField(nid, "frame="); }
  /// "windowGroup=<WindowGroup>": which acquisition group this frame belongs to. A group holds each
  /// isolation m/z at most once, so (m/z, group) names one ion-mobility slice of the window; the
  /// scan range alone does not (two slices can share ScanNumBegin = 0 and differ in ScanNumEnd).
  /// 0 when absent (mzPeak input), which keeps that path keyed by m/z alone, as before.
  inline uint32_t windowGroupOf(const String& nid) { return nidField(nid, "windowGroup="); }

  /// [tile-2d] One frame's picked peaks written into a caller-provided region sized by the raw count
  /// (a pick never emits more points). No per-frame allocation: vectors allocated on picking threads
  /// and freed on the main thread strand free-list in their arenas.
  size_t compactifyInto(const MSSpectrum& s, CompactStats& st, uint32_t* mzq, float* inten, uint16_t* imq, size_t room)
  {
    if (!s.containsIMData()) { st.no_im_array += s.size(); return 0; }
    const auto& im = s.getFloatDataArrays()[s.getIMData().first];
    const Size n = s.size();
    if (im.size() != n) { st.size_mismatch += n; return 0; }   // no out-of-bounds read
    if (n > room) throw OpenMS::Exception::InvalidValue(__FILE__, __LINE__, OPENMS_PRETTY_FUNCTION, "picked frame larger than its raw region", std::to_string(n));
    size_t k = 0;
    for (Size i = 0; i < n; ++i)
    {
      const double mz = s[i].getMZ();
      const double imv = (double)im[i];
      if (!std::isfinite(mz) || mz <= 0.0 || mz * MZ_Q > 4.2e9) { ++st.bad_mz; continue; }
      if (!std::isfinite(imv) || imv < IM_LO || imv > IM_HI)    { ++st.bad_im; continue; }
      mzq[k] = (uint32_t)llround(mz * MZ_Q);
      inten[k] = s[i].getIntensity();
      imq[k] = (uint16_t)llround((imv - IM_LO) * IM_Q);
      ++k; ++st.kept;
    }
    return k;
  }

  /// [det] Order-insensitive 64-bit digest of a stage's numeric output, for localising
Good — the "drops and counts (bad_mz)" claim is real: `compactifyInto` drops non-finite m/z peaks (`!std::isfinite(mz) || mz <= 0.0 || mz*MZ_Q > 4.2e9`) and counts them. So a NaN m/z from the loader (negative-C2 file, TOF beyond the parabola peak) is dropped and counted there. But that's the compact-store path — is that the only path? The OpenMS detector path (non-integer) — do NaN peaks flow into spectra? Line 1370 Counts has bad_mz too, and 1418 "never reaches this line and bad_mz stays 0". Let me check that context briefly. Actually, that's the dnoise per-frame counts. The key: with dnoise:ms1 true, recoverPoint throws on NaN m/z (Refusal::tof) — fail closed. With dnoise off and integer detector, compactify drops NaN. With dnoise off and OpenMS detector — the spectrum goes to MassTrace detection; NaN m/z peaks... whatever OpenMS does. Pre-existing hazard for any NaN m/z (could already happen with C2 ≥ 0 for t ≤ C0 bins? tofToMz returns NaN for tof with t ≤ C0 — tof index 0..(C0-delay)/tb ≈ (279-25655)/0.125 → negative, so no real bin maps to NaN for C2 ≥ 0; for C2 < 0 bins beyond vertex → NaN, only possible for corrupt blob indices). OK.

  Now the C++ standard is C++20 → static constexpr members fine.

  Line 3273: dnoise setup calls loadTofAxis — so with default dnoise:ms1 true, the tool's reader (with frameReason) runs ✓. Line 3982: integer detector load. So paths covered.

  Now check the OpenMS detector + dnoise-off path: loader converts with unchecked T1. That's the gap.

  Let me also double check the mixed-deploy claim and whether TdfLoad.h includes <OpenMS/FORMAT/TdfMzCalibration.h> — interesting: TdfLoad.h includes the OpenMS-installed copy, not src/TdfMzCalibration.h! Line 4: `#include <OpenMS/FORMAT/TdfMzCalibration.h> // the copy the OpenMS patch installs; same header as src/TdfMzCalibration.h`. So the tool compiles against the INSTALLED copy in the OpenMS tree. If the installed copy is the OLD header (mixed deploy), the tool's TdfMzCalibration is the old one: no frameReason method → compile error when building the tool. Actually scripts/sync_cluster.sh rebuilds libOpenMS and installs the header then builds the tool, per section F description. But standalone build against an installed OpenMS with an old header → TdfLoad.h would fail to compile (cal.frameReason missing) — a loud failure, fail-safe. And the digest pins: harness test 1 includes... The "shipped code is tested code" invariant: test_calibration_cpp.cpp includes which header? Check: does it include src/TdfMzCalibration.h or the OpenMS copy? Let me check.Ich habe bestätigt, dass `compactifyInto` nicht‑finite m/z‑Werte loswirft und zählt, und prüfe nun, ob NaN‑Peaks auch in den OpenMS‑Detector‑Pfad gelangen.Ich prüfe, ob dnoise ms1 true führt zu einem Fehler bei NaN‑m/z, und ob bei dnoise aus‑Und OpenMS‑Detektor NaN‑Peaks protokolliert oder weggeworfen werden.Ich überprüfe, ob `C2 < 0` zu NaN-Werten führt und verfolge, dass dies nur bei korrupten Blob‑Indices vorkommt.  
  Dann stelle ich sicher, dass die C++20‑Standardnutzung mit `static constexpr` korrekt ist, erkenne an den Zeilennummern, wann `loadTofAxis` und der Integer‑Detektor aufgerufen werden, und prüfe, ob die OpenMS‑Detektor‑Pfade bei deaktiviertem dnoise korrekt behandelt werden.  
  Schließlich plane ich, die mixed‑Deploy‑Aussage zu prüfen und festzustellen, ob `TdfLoad.h` die richtige OpenMS‑Header‑Einbindung verwendet.Ich prüfe, dass `TdfLoad.h` das in OpenMS installierte Headerfile benutzt, nicht die lokale Version. Dadurch kompiliert das Tool gegen die alte Headerkopie, die keine `frameReason`-Methode enthält, was zu einem Buildfehler führt. Das Skript `sync_cluster.sh` baut libOpenMS neu, installiert das Header und baut anschließend das Tool.Ziel ist es zu prüfen, ob die Tests die lokale Headerdatei nutzen und ob ein Build mit einer alten OpenMS‑Version scheitert, weil cal.frameReason fehlt.4:#include "TdfMzCalibration.h"
