  Now I have everything. Let me also double-check one Q1 sub-claim: "continuous through C2 = 0 and C2 = -0.0". C2 = -0.0: disc = b² - 4·(-0.0)·(C0-t). -4·(-0.0) = +0.0; +0.0·(C0-t) = ±0.0 (sign depends). b² + 0.0 = b² exactly (IEEE: x + 0 = x for x ≠ -0; x + (-0) = x too). So disc bit-identical to C2 = +0.0 case (b² - 0.0·(...) = b² - ±0 = b²). u = 2(t-C0)/(b + sqrt(b²)) — identical. ✓ And -0.0 < 0.0 is false → no domain check → accepted. Test covers it ✓.

  Also Q1: could the code return the falling-branch root? No — derived above, u = 2(t-C0)/(b+sqrt(disc)) is algebraically the rising root (b - sqrt(disc))/(2|C2|) for C2 < 0. Negative u impossible since numerator > 0 (t > C0) and denominator > 0 (b > 0, sqrt ≥ 0). Finite wrong value: none — at disc = 0 exactly (t = vertex), u = 2(t-C0)/(2b) = (t-C0)/b = b/(2|C2|) = u_v, the double root — correct value, m = u_v² - C4, the maximum m/z. Fine.

