  Similarly `best`'s hi_mz = cmz + 3sd ≤ ~42950 + 3sd — always ≪ vertex for accepted cals (vertex m ≥ 4e5 + 3C4; C4 ≥ -... unbounded C4! If C4 = -3e5: vertex m = 4u_half² - C4 ≥ 4e5 + 3·(-3e5)... wait the domain check: 1e5 + C4 ≤ u_half². With C4 = -3e5: u_half² ≥ -2e5 trivially true. m_vertex = u_v² - C4 = 4u_half² + 3e5. u_half could be small if... no wait, u_half = b/k can be anything ≥ 0. If C2 barely fails the t-side? No — both conditions must pass. With C4 very negative, the m/z condition is vacuous, and u_half can be arbitrarily small... but then the t-side: t_max ≤ t_half still required, and t_half = C0 + 0.75b²/k. Hmm, the t-side binds k: k ≤ 0.75b²/(t_max - C0). u_half = b/k ≥ b·(t_max-C0)/(0.75b²) = (t_max-C0)/(0.75b) — for PXD-like: (2e7)/(0.75·2516) ≈ 1.06e4 → u_half² ≈ 1.1e8. So u_half² is bounded below by the t-side condition: u_half² ≥ (t_max - C0)²/(0.5625 b²) — always large (~1e8) for PXD-like timebase. But with a fine timebase (1 ps), t_max - C0 ≈ 25780+1e5-C0... small t_max → t-side weak → u_half² could be small → then the m/z side binds: u_half² ≥ 1e5 + C4. So m_vertex = 4u_half² - C4 ≥ 4(1e5 + C4) - C4 = 4e5 + 3C4. With C4 = -1e5: m_vertex ≥ 1e5. cmz ≤ 42950 + 3sd... could 42950 > m_vertex = 1e5? No, 42950 < 1e5. But sd could push hi_mz above 1e5? sd is the robust SD of accepted peaks — bounded by the m/z spread of peaks in a window, realistically ≤ 100. Theoretically unbounded though. If hi_mz > m_vertex, tofOf returns a bin on the... mzToTof(mz > m_vertex) returns t on the falling side: t < t_vertex → a SMALLER bin → hi_t collapses → the ±3sd bin window is wrong → the walk misses candidates → wrong trace/peak assignment, silently. But requires: accepted negative-C2 calibration with small vertex (fine timebase or near m/z-edge) AND huge sd AND store m/z near the cap. Deeply theoretical. LOW, mention briefly.

