  Severity assessment of the loader-T1 gap: MEDIUM / theoretical-only (requires corrupt T1 beyond ±1000 K — no real file; and even then with |dC1| ≤ 1000 the m/z error is bounded by dC1·ΔT ppm which for absurd T1 could be large: T1 = -1e6, dC1 = 999: cf = 1 + 999·(1e6)/1e6 ≈ 1000 → m/z error ~ (1 - 1/sqrt(1000)) ≈ 3% = 30000 ppm! Wait — that's a big error: m ∝ C1_eff⁻¹... m/z scales as 1/cf... Actually b ∝ 1/sqrt(cf), u ≈ (t-C0)/b ∝ sqrt(cf), m ∝ cf. So cf = 1000 → m/z 1000× too large. But hold on — the loader checks isSupported() which for negative C2 requires cf_lo > 0 i.e. |dC1| < 1000 ppm/K. dC1 = 999 passes. A corrupt Frames.T1 = -1e6 °C would then convert peaks at 1000× the true m/z — plausible-looking? No — m/z 100000-1.7e6, all beyond acquisition range, MS1 finite-check passes (finite), MS2 compactify: mz·MZ_Q > 4.2e9 → dropped as bad_mz ✓. MS1: dnoise recoverPoint would refuse (not a bin's model value at the loader's b... wait recoverPoint uses the tool's own axis b from loadTdfCalibration — which WOULD refuse the file first (frameReason) when dnoise runs. With dnoise off and openms detector: MS1 spectra with m/z ~ 1e5-1.7e6 — finite, positive → requireFiniteMs1Mz passes → MassTrace detection on garbage m/z → wrong output silently! Hmm. But MS2 compactify drops them (bad_mz). MS1 map: m/z 1.7e6 in MS1 spectra... the MS1 map/feature detection — would produce garbage features at absurd m/z. Is there any guard on MS1 m/z range? acq bounds mz_hi ≤ 1e5 enforced via loadTdfAxisBounds only in integer-detector paths. With openms detector + dnoise off, MS1 garbage flows. But this needs a *corrupt* file (T1 = -1e6 °C) — and for C2 ≥ 0 files the same corrupt T1 converts with the same wrong cf today (pre-existing, unchanged by this branch; the branch's frameReason only adds the check for negative C2 in the tool's reader). So the marginal risk introduced by 1.3.1: negative-C2 files newly accepted can now carry this corrupt-T1 hazard through the loader-only path, where before they were refused outright. That's a real (if theoretical) regression of the fail-closed posture for exactly the newly-accepted class. MEDIUM, theoretical. This is the strongest finding for Q4/Q9.

  Also for the digest-gate claim (invariant 2): CHANGELOG asserts pins reproduce before the gate ran — the claim "by construction" is solid (I verified: no changed line touches C2 ≥ 0 conversions; the only behavioral change for those files is check order in unsupportedReason — and no accepted file hits it). Still, stating it as fact pre-gate is a process finding. LOW-MEDIUM.

  One more: harness/critical_guards.txt — do the guards need updating for the new lines? Q7 mentions provenance. Let me check quickly if critical_guards.txt references the old refusal string "negative quadratic term is not a supported model" — that string is now GONE from the header; if a guard greps for it, CI breaks. Check.I notice that the C++ test never parses `C4`, so it defaults to 0.0, which could become a problem if future golden files contain a non‑zero value; `num_samples` is likewise unused. I’m also concerned that `nextNumber(j,"C0",…)` searches with a substring, potentially matching unintended keys (for example, “C1” could be found inside “dC1”), so I’ll inspect the `nextNumber` implementation.The analysis confirms that `nextNumber` looks for the key wrapped in quotes (`"C1":`), so it won’t mistakenly match it inside a longer key such as `"dC1":`. The function then parses the keys in order, advancing the pointer after each successful extraction.I see the parser correctly advances past the C1 value and then finds the C2 key; I proceed to verify that the discriminant remains unchanged for C2 = 0 and C2 = –0.0, confirming continuity.It confirms that –0.0 passes the domain check and the test succeeds, and explains that the solver can’t return the falling‑branch root—only the rising root, giving the correct double root when the discriminant is zero.I examine whether the refusal message could produce garbage from the num() %.6g calculation on the m/z at half‑slope; the u_half² – C4 term stays finite. I also consider any subnormal values when cf_lo > 0, but the intermediate b remains well above 0.7, so all numbers are finite. Finally, I note that the t_zero term will appear in the refusal message under the specified condition.Checking that the assertion message cites the huge TOF index and m/z, and inspecting the Q3 edge‑case calculation for a negative‑C2 calibration.It returns about 1×10⁸, which is below the 4 294 967 294 limit, so no exception is thrown. That m/z (~1.1×10⁸) is well above the trusted domain maximum of 1×10⁵, placing the computation in a mathematically valid but unverified regime – thus the output remains correct but unchecked.I note that this scenario falls outside the defined domain yet remains mathematically sound, because correctness only relies on monotonicity up to four times the half‑slope mass. The hi_mz bound for a best estimate is far below the vertex even with extreme C4 values, though the exact reasoning for the C4 bounds is still being finalized.Analyzing how an extreme negative C4 makes the m/z check trivial, leaving only the t‑side to constrain k and u_half.The estimate puts \(u_{\text{half}}^{2}\) near \(10^{8}\), which is bounded below by the t‑side condition. With a fine timebase this t‑side bound can become weak, so the m/z side enforces \(u_{\text{half}}^{2}\ge10^{5}+C_{4}\), giving a vertex mass \(\ge4\times10^{5}+3C_{4}\) and, for \(C_{4}=-10^{5}\), at least \(10^{5}\), while the \(m_{\text{cz}}\) upper bound (~\(4.3\times10^{4}\)) remains below this vertex.Only in extreme, unlikely cases where a negative‑C₂ calibration yields a very high m/z (above 1 × 10⁵) could the mapping to a falling‑side bin break the ±3 sd window, though recoverPoint stays correct because the loader always outputs a rising‑side m/z.I’m looking at how `tofToMz` behaves when the bin time exceeds the vertex— it should return NaN and cause an early refuse, so recoverPoint remains safe because the loader only outputs rising‑branch m/z. I also want to inspect `mzToTof` near `m_vertex` to ensure the uint32 cast doesn’t wrap for large m/z when `C2 ≥ 0`, and then confirm the high‑TOF‐index test and the 1100 °C refused‑frame message.The test verifies that frame 1100 is within 1000 K of the calibration temperature 25.6188 K. The changelog notes that each test file is now checked independently and that or negating C2 must shift the result by at least 5 ppm; both conditions are satisfied, and the C++ test also checks round‑trip binning to 1 e‑6 and temperature ablation. I will next confirm the sign‑flip subtlety in the Python twin.I calculated that when C₂ is negative the standard quadratic root still returns the rising‑branch solution, so the flipped calibration still produces a valid m/z.I checked the Python implementation of `tof_to_mz` with a flipped negative C₂: the discriminant stays positive even for the largest TOF values, so the test that asserts it > 0 passes. The golden JSON for this case omits a C₄ key, and the Python code handles that correctly.Confirming default C4 is 0.0, num_samples ignored; validating that the 210 cases use vendor tims_index_to_mz and noting potential issues with an empty tdf_bin.The test passed (rc = 1) and the worst ppm error of 7 × 10⁻⁶ confirms the values align with the independently validated model. In the dense‑sweep, the `worst` data is reset inside each run loop and NaNs are counted per run. All seven probes (2000 and N‑1) lie above the low‑TOF threshold, so every bin’s time exceeds C₀, eliminating a low‑side risk for these files.I’m planning to rank Q9 and flag a few fixes: make sure the digest gate runs before release (invariant 2), consider extending the known SHA256 list to all six runs, add a per‑file temperature‑ablation guard or drop the claim, move the frameReason into the OpenMS loader patch (or explain why it stays), note the stale “[2, 1e8]” literal in TdfLoad.h, and include a C4 key in the golden schema. The loader‑T1 gap is rated medium and purely theoretical, needing a corrupt T1 beyond ±1000 K—which never happens—and even then |dC1| ≤ 1000 would still prevent the problem.A very large temperature offset combined with a large dC1 could produce a ~30 000 ppm m/z error (≈ 3 %). However, the loader’s isSupported() check ensures |dC1| stays below 1000 ppm/K, so even a wildly negative T1 would only scale m/z by about 1000×.The m/z values (≈100 k–1.7 M) exceed the acquisition range—MS1 passes the finite check but MS2 compactification marks them as bad, and recoverPoint with dnoise would reject the file during loading. With dnoise off and using the OpenMS detector, the spectra still pass finite checks, so MassTrace detection would run on the invalid data.I note that while the MS2 filter removes the bad m/z values, the MS1 mapping could still produce absurd 1.7 M m/z features if no guard is present; this guard is only enforced in integer‑detector paths, so with the openMS detector and noise‑calibration off a corrupt file (T1 = ‑1 M °C) can yield the wrong conversion factor that remains unchanged for C2 ≥ 0.I see that negative‑C₂ files, once rejected, are now accepted, which could let a corrupt T₁ slip through the loader path—a medium‑level theoretical regression. The digest‑gate claim about processing order remains valid. I also note that the guard list may fail because the old refusal string has been removed.

